    <title>Problems</title>
    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Memory loss and confusion over about a year, worse in recent months, reported by him and his care partner: repeating stories and questions, losing the thread of conversations, mixing up days, two missed appointments, and once getting lost changing trains on a familiar route; cause to be found</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R41.3</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting problem anchored to the documented short-term memory problems; resolved into the diagnosis at the third visit. Not a claim about his real symptoms at that age.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Post-traumatic stress disorder, chronic, after two index events (a title fight decades ago after which his opponent died, and a severe assault thirteen years ago): nightmares, unwanted images, avoidance of fight footage, guilt and self-blame, being on guard in crowds, poor sleep; PCL-5 41</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>F43.12</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis at intake by clinical interview against DSM-5-TR criteria, anchored to his public words about lifelong nightmares; not a historical diagnosis.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Mild cognitive impairment (mild neurocognitive disorder) with slowed processing, executive and memory-retrieval deficits, most likely due to traumatic brain injury from a nineteen-year professional boxing career and a severe assault; meets the NINDS research criteria for traumatic encephalopathy syndrome (a research diagnosis); Alzheimer&apos;s disease co-pathology not excluded</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>G31.84</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis at the third visit after MRI and neuropsychological testing; anchored to the documented memory problems and pugilistic dementia, not a retrospective diagnosis of his real illness.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-4">
    <td>Personal history of traumatic brain injury: repetitive head impacts over 112 professional bouts, and a severe head injury in an assault thirteen years ago with months in hospital</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>Z87.820</td><td>S — Synthetic coding of the documented career and assault.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

