    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Mild cognitive impairment, most likely due to traumatic brain injury (G31.84)</content>. Testing, the MRI and the history fit mild cognitive impairment, most likely due to traumatic brain injury from repetitive head impacts and the severe assault, with PTSD, depressive symptoms and poor sleep adding to the difficulty. He meets the NINDS research criteria for traumatic encephalopathy syndrome; this was explained as a research label that cannot confirm the brain changes in life and does not change treatment. Alzheimer&apos;s disease is less likely given the retrieval pattern and normal hippocampi; biomarker testing was explained, and he chose not to have it now because it would not change his care (with seven microbleeds he would not be a candidate for anti-amyloid antibodies). The memory loss and confusion that brought him in are resolved into this diagnosis. [S]<list><item>No cholinesterase inhibitor or memantine.</item><item>One calendar, phone reminders and a fixed daily routine.</item><item>Exercise, social activity and sleep as the measures most likely to help.</item><item>Repeat neuropsychological testing in twelve to eighteen months, or sooner with change.</item><item>Card with his address and his care partner&apos;s number; shared phone location; company on unfamiliar routes; shared oversight of bills.</item><item>Alzheimer&apos;s Association helpline and caregiver class for his care partner.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Post-traumatic stress disorder, chronic (F43.12)</content>. Early improvement (PCL-5 37 from 41) after three therapy sessions and six weeks of sertraline 25 mg, which is well tolerated. No thoughts of suicide. [S]<list><item>Raise sertraline to 50 mg each morning.</item><item>Sodium check two to four weeks after the increase.</item><item>Continue weekly cognitive processing therapy.</item><item>Safety plan and 988 reviewed.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Cognitive testing (MoCA)</td><td>At diagnosis, then yearly, or sooner with change</td><td>Up to date</td><td>MoCA 22 at intake; next in about a year</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Staging (CDR) and function (ADL and IADL)</td><td>At diagnosis, then yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Global CDR 0.5 at two weeks; function unchanged</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Reversible causes: blood count, metabolic panel, B12, TSH, depression, sleep apnea, hearing and vision</td><td>Once at diagnosis</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Completed at intake and two weeks; no reversible cause found beyond PTSD, depressive symptoms and poor sleep, which are being treated</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Structural brain MRI</td><td>Once at diagnosis; repeat only with an atypical course or focal change</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Done at two weeks; no repeat needed</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Neuropsychological testing</td><td>At diagnosis when the cause or stage is unclear; repeat in 12 to 18 months or with change</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Testing a week ago reviewed today: slowed processing, reduced set-shifting and fluency, retrieval better with cues; mild cognitive impairment of frontal-subcortical type</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Alzheimer&apos;s disease biomarkers (blood, CSF or amyloid PET)</td><td>When the result would change diagnosis or management</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Explained and declined for now; would not change his care, and seven microbleeds would exclude anti-amyloid antibodies</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Medicine review for anticholinergic, sedating and fall-risk drugs (Beers)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Sertraline only; no cholinesterase inhibitor started (no benefit shown in mild cognitive impairment)</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Neuropsychiatric symptom screen (apathy, irritability, paranoia, impulsivity)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No apathy, irritability, paranoia or impulsive behavior</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9 with suicide-risk review (item 9 and structured questions)</td><td>Monthly during acute treatment; every visit during active treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-9 8, item 9 scored 0; no thoughts of suicide</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PCL-5</td><td>Baseline, then at each chronic-care visit during treatment; response judged after 8 to 12 weeks</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PCL-5 37 (41 at intake)</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Sertraline: nausea, sleep, falls, bleeding, and sodium (SSRIs can lower sodium in older adults)</td><td>Two weeks after starting and after each dose change; sodium at those points</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Well tolerated at 25 mg; raised to 50 mg today; sodium due in two to four weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Trauma-focused psychotherapy (cognitive processing therapy)</td><td>Weekly, about twelve sessions</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Three of twelve sessions done, adapted with one-page summaries</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Fall risk (Timed Up and Go, gait and balance), STEADI</td><td>At diagnosis, yearly, and after any fall</td><td>Up to date</td><td>No falls since intake</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Physical therapy for balance and strength</td><td>Once, after a Timed Up and Go above 12 seconds</td><td>Referred</td><td>Rebooked evaluation next week, with a reminder call</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Travel, money and home safety</td><td>At diagnosis and each follow-up</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Address card, shared phone location, company on unfamiliar routes and shared oversight of bills agreed</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Care partner support and burden (Zarit Burden Interview, narrative)</td><td>At diagnosis, then every 6 to 12 months</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Zarit Burden Interview 14 (little to mild burden); helpline, caregiver class and GUIDE model explained for if dementia develops</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Advance care planning</td><td>At diagnosis; revisit yearly and at changes</td><td>Due</td><td>He wants to name a health care agent soon; booked for the annual wellness visit in seven weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Vascular risk factors (blood pressure, weight)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Blood pressure 128/78 mmHg; weight 73.6 kg</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Find the cause of the memory problems and explain it to him in a way he can take in. Outcome by 2026-06-16: Mild cognitive impairment most likely due to traumatic brain injury; explained with his care partner present, at his request, with a written summary [S]</item><item>A clinically meaningful fall in PTSD symptoms (PCL-5 down at least 10 points) by four months. In progress; target 2026-09-15 [S]</item><item>Nightmares no more than about once a week, with at least six hours of sleep most nights. In progress; target 2026-10-27 [S]</item><item>No falls, with a balance and strength program. In progress; target 2026-10-27 [S]</item><item>Name a health care agent and record his goals of care while he can take part fully. In progress; target 2026-08-04 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Alzheimer&apos;s Association clinical practice guideline for the Diagnostic Evaluation, Testing, Counseling, and Disclosure of Suspected Alzheimer&apos;s Disease and Related Disorders (DETeCD-ADRD): Executive summary of recommendations for specialty care (2024); National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke Consensus Diagnostic Criteria for Traumatic Encephalopathy Syndrome (2021); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Management of Posttraumatic Stress Disorder and Acute Stress Disorder (2023); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, Non-Alzheimer&apos;s dementia and Depression/Bipolar/Mood Disorders monographs (2026); American Geriatrics Society 2023 updated AGS Beers Criteria for potentially inappropriate medication use in older adults (2023); Guiding an Improved Dementia Experience (GUIDE) Model (2024)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Physical therapy evaluation next week. Annual wellness visit in seven weeks. Four-month review with PCL-5, PHQ-9 and sodium. Call the clinic or 988 at any time if thoughts of suicide arise. Next scheduled visit 2026-08-04: subsequent annual wellness visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

